1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,041
The brush uncovers a temple tower, tier stacked on tier.

2
00:00:04,041 --> 00:00:10,624
The great one at Thanjavur stands about sixty metres high and its tiers are built of stone fitted without mortar.

3
00:00:14,033 --> 00:00:20,315
Building it needed quarries, transport, engineering and money — which means it also needed an administration,

4
00:00:20,315 --> 00:00:23,242
and that is the real subject of this episode.

5
00:00:25,833 --> 00:00:31,110
The Cholas are the best-documented ancient Indian state, because they wrote on walls.

6
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:38,351
Thousands of inscriptions are carved on temple surfaces, recording grants, disputes, assembly decisions and accounts.

7
00:00:38,351 --> 00:00:43,192
Add excavation, Tamil literature, and Chinese records of their embassies.

8
00:00:46,133 --> 00:00:51,893
The dynasty rises from about 850 CE under Vijayalaya and reaches its height under two rulers.

9
00:00:51,893 --> 00:00:58,994
Rajaraja the First, who built the Brihadisvara temple at Thanjavur and conducted a survey of land for revenue.

10
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:07,621
And his son Rajendra the First, who campaigned north to the Ganga and founded a new capital,

11
00:01:07,621 --> 00:01:12,419
Gangaikonda Cholapuram — a name that means the city of the Chola who took the Ganga.

12
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:21,502
And then something no other Indian dynasty of this period did: they built a navy and used it.

13
00:01:21,502 --> 00:01:26,769
Rajendra sent a naval expedition against Srivijaya, the maritime power of Southeast Asia,

14
00:01:26,769 --> 00:01:30,991
and the Cholas maintained trade and diplomatic contact with China.

15
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:39,194
Historians debate the aims — protecting merchant guilds' access to eastern

16
00:01:39,194 --> 00:01:43,698
trade is the usual explanation — but the expedition itself is well attested.

17
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:51,113
Now the administration, which is what examiners ask about.

18
00:01:51,113 --> 00:01:53,063
Three kinds of local assembly.

19
00:01:53,063 --> 00:01:56,698
The ur, the general assembly of an ordinary village.

20
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:07,057
The sabha, the assembly of a brahmadeya, a village granted to Brahmins.

21
00:02:07,057 --> 00:02:10,013
And the nagaram, the assembly of a merchant town.

22
00:02:10,013 --> 00:02:15,516
Their proceedings were inscribed on temple walls, which is why we know how they worked.

23
00:02:17,633 --> 00:02:27,827
One inscription in particular, from Uttaramerur, describes the sabha's procedure in detail: the village divided into wards, candidates qualified by property, age,

24
00:02:27,827 --> 00:02:32,079
literacy and conduct, names written on palm leaves, put into a pot,

25
00:02:32,079 --> 00:02:39,195
and a ticket drawn out — the kudavolai system — with committees formed for gardens, tanks, gold and justice.

26
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,759
It also disqualifies anyone who has not submitted accounts.

27
00:02:44,759 --> 00:02:50,938
Whether every village worked this way is unknown; that one village wrote its rules on a wall is certain.

28
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:57,058
How centralised was it?

29
00:02:57,058 --> 00:03:03,742
One view reads the surveys, the inscriptions and the campaigns as evidence of a genuinely centralised empire.

30
00:03:03,742 --> 00:03:06,050
Another, influential since the 1980s,

31
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:14,370
describes it as a segmentary state — the king's authority ritual and symbolic at a distance, with real administration done locally.

32
00:03:17,633 --> 00:03:25,724
A third position sits between them, accepting strong local institutions alongside effective central demands for revenue and soldiers.

33
00:03:25,724 --> 00:03:28,862
Know all three and the evidence each leans on.

34
00:03:31,733 --> 00:03:34,828
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:34,828 --> 00:03:36,791
Ur, the ordinary village.

36
00:03:36,791 --> 00:03:38,904
Sabha, the Brahmin village.

37
00:03:38,904 --> 00:03:41,318
Nagaram, the merchant town.

38
00:03:46,167 --> 00:03:46,820
Dig Quiz.

39
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:48,060
Pause and name it.

40
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:56,327
First: the procedure, described at Uttaramerur, by which names on palm leaves were drawn from a pot to select committees.

41
00:03:59,567 --> 00:04:01,040
The kudavolai system.

42
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:05,125
Second: the temple built at Thanjavur by Rajaraja the First.

43
00:04:05,125 --> 00:04:07,099
The Brihadisvara temple.

44
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:15,405
Third: the Southeast Asian maritime power against which Rajendra sent a naval expedition.

45
00:04:15,405 --> 00:04:16,139
Srivijaya.

46
00:04:16,139 --> 00:04:21,109
In Mains, a frequent question asks about Chola local self-government.

47
00:04:23,733 --> 00:04:28,214
Describe the three assemblies and the Uttaramerur procedure, then be careful:

48
00:04:28,214 --> 00:04:33,224
one inscription is evidence of a system's ideal, not proof of universal practice.

49
00:04:35,567 --> 00:04:41,116
A temple tower takes its place in the gallery, built by a state that wrote its own accounts on the walls.

50
00:04:44,533 --> 00:04:51,882
Next gallery: the other kingdoms of the Deccan and the south, and the most intricate stone carving anybody in India attempted.

51
00:04:51,882 --> 00:04:55,481
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

